1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:03,640
Police non-emergency lines spend a lot of their time

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:05,150
on routine questions.

3
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:07,450
How do I report a stolen bike?

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:09,110
Where do I get a record check?

5
00:00:09,953 --> 00:00:12,493
I built a small voice agent on ElevenLabs with

6
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:15,113
Claude Code as my coding assistant to see how

7
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:16,523
much of that it could handle.

8
00:00:17,182 --> 00:00:19,582
The agent's knowledge is seventeen public pages from the

9
00:00:19,602 --> 00:00:23,062
Toronto Police website: how to report online, collision reporting,

10
00:00:23,082 --> 00:00:24,472
record checks, and so on.

11
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:26,702
It's told to answer only from those pages and

12
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:27,692
to say which one.

13
00:00:28,533 --> 00:00:30,533
Here's a caller who says only that someone hit

14
00:00:30,553 --> 00:00:31,183
their car.

15
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:32,783
The agent doesn't guess

16
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:34,790
Caller: Somebody hit my car

17
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:37,590
Agent: Is anyone injured?

18
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:40,630
Caller: No, nobody seems to be injured

19
00:00:41,247 --> 00:00:44,167
It asks about injuries first, then works through what

20
00:00:44,187 --> 00:00:47,307
it needs and answers from the published guidance, naming

21
00:00:47,327 --> 00:00:48,037
its source

22
00:00:48,907 --> 00:00:51,847
Agent: According to the Toronto Police Collision Reporting page, if

23
00:00:51,867 --> 00:00:53,547
no one is injured and the other driver is

24
00:00:53,567 --> 00:00:55,577
present, you should exchange information.

25
00:00:55,787 --> 00:00:58,287
This includes the location of the collision, the license

26
00:00:58,307 --> 00:01:00,907
plate number, year, make, model, and color of the

27
00:01:00,927 --> 00:01:01,637
other vehicle

28
00:01:02,433 --> 00:01:04,973
When the caller wants a real person, it takes

29
00:01:04,993 --> 00:01:07,393
a callback request, and that lands in the team

30
00:01:07,413 --> 00:01:09,323
channel in Mattermost a moment later

31
00:01:10,213 --> 00:01:12,633
Agent: So to confirm, your first name is Alex, and

32
00:01:12,653 --> 00:01:15,293
the callback number is four one six five five

33
00:01:15,313 --> 00:01:16,663
five zero one four two.

34
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:18,443
The callback is about a car accident.

35
00:01:18,613 --> 00:01:19,503
Is that all correct?

36
00:01:20,473 --> 00:01:21,203
Caller: That's correct.

37
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:23,543
Agent: Your callback request has been queued.

38
00:01:23,813 --> 00:01:26,053
Your reference number is two E zero six F

39
00:01:26,073 --> 00:01:26,543
five.

40
00:01:26,713 --> 00:01:29,053
Please note that this is a demonstration, so no

41
00:01:29,073 --> 00:01:31,403
one from Toronto Police will actually call you back

42
00:01:33,063 --> 00:01:35,353
The most important behavior is knowing when to stop.

43
00:01:35,463 --> 00:01:37,623
Here, the call starts out vague, and the moment

44
00:01:37,643 --> 00:01:39,703
it sounds like someone is in danger, the agent

45
00:01:39,723 --> 00:01:41,683
says to hang up and call nine one one

46
00:01:41,703 --> 00:01:42,773
and does nothing else

47
00:01:43,573 --> 00:01:45,343
Caller: There's somebody outside my house.

48
00:01:46,753 --> 00:01:48,063
Agent: Is anyone in danger right now?

49
00:01:49,693 --> 00:01:50,943
Caller: I think they're trying to get in.

50
00:01:52,473 --> 00:01:54,263
Agent: Please hang up and call 911 now

51
00:01:55,913 --> 00:01:58,333
The knowledge base, the agent, its webhook tool, and

52
00:01:58,353 --> 00:02:00,873
its tests are all defined in Python and kept

53
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:01,943
in version control.

54
00:02:02,253 --> 00:02:05,013
The tests include simulated callers that hold a whole

55
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:05,883
conversation.

56
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:09,333
Every test runs three times because a single passing

57
00:02:09,353 --> 00:02:11,473
run turned out to prove very little, and the

58
00:02:11,493 --> 00:02:14,053
first unscripted question I asked it by voice still

59
00:02:14,073 --> 00:02:16,233
found a false alarm that none of those tests

60
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:17,123
had a case for.

61
00:02:19,633 --> 00:02:23,393
For organizations with strict privacy requirements, here's what this

62
00:02:23,413 --> 00:02:25,183
design gives you to work with.

63
00:02:26,072 --> 00:02:28,562
The agent's knowledge is seventeen public webpages.

64
00:02:28,592 --> 00:02:30,142
Nothing confidential goes in.

65
00:02:31,011 --> 00:02:33,491
A callback sends three things to your systems, a

66
00:02:33,511 --> 00:02:36,281
first name, a phone number, and a one-line summary.

67
00:02:37,084 --> 00:02:40,144
Zero retention mode limits what ElevenLabs keeps from each

68
00:02:40,164 --> 00:02:40,554
call

69
00:02:41,406 --> 00:02:44,066
Regional data residency keeps stored data in a chosen

70
00:02:44,086 --> 00:02:45,296
region where it's offered

71
00:02:46,165 --> 00:02:48,605
And for anything sensitive, the model itself can run

72
00:02:48,625 --> 00:02:49,365
on your side.

73
00:02:49,385 --> 00:02:51,415
So ElevenLabs handles only the speech

74
00:02:57,980 --> 00:03:00,410
The code and a one-page write-up are linked below.

75
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:01,250
Thanks for watching.

